.TH A.OUT 5
.UC 2
.SH NAME
a.out \- assembler and link editor output for the 80286
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <a.out.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I A.out
is the output file of the assembler
.IR as (1)
and the link editor
.IR ld (1).
The assembler writes an object file, and the link editor makes a
program of object files, which it makes executable if there were no
errors and no unresolved external references.
Both are of one format.
Layout information as given in the include file for the 80286 is:
.PP
.nf
.ta \w'#define  'u +\w'unsigned short  'u +\w'xs_filesz;  'u
/*
 * Header prepended to each a.out file.
 */
struct	exec {
	unsigned short	a_magic;	/* magic number */
	unsigned short	a_model;	/* memory model */
	unsigned short	a_nseg;	/* number of segments */
	unsigned short	a_eseg;	/* entry point: segment, */
	unsigned short	a_entry;	/*	and offset */
	unsigned short	a_dseg;	/* the segment DS and SS start as */
	unsigned short	a_stack;	/* size of the stack */
	unsigned short	a_unused;	/* not used */
	unsigned long	a_syms;	/* size of symbol table */
	unsigned long	a_strs;	/* size of string table */
};

struct	exseg {
	unsigned short	xs_type;	/* XS_CODE or XS_DATA */
	unsigned short	xs_nrel;	/* entries of relocation */
	unsigned long	xs_filesz;	/* bytes of it in the file */
	unsigned long	xs_memsz;	/* bytes in memory */
};

#define	NXSEG	31
struct	xexec {
	struct exec e;
	struct exseg x[NXSEG];
};

#define	A_MAGIC	0x0286	/* a program */
#define	A_OBJECT	0x0186	/* an object file */

#define	A_SMALL	1	/* one code segment, near data */
#define	A_MEDIUM	2	/* many code segments, near data */

#define	XS_CODE	1
#define	XS_DATA	2

/*
 * An object file's relocation.
 */
struct	reloc {
	unsigned short	r_addr;	/* where the word is in its segment */
	unsigned short	r_sym;	/* a symbol, or a segment */
	unsigned short	r_type;
};

#define	R_OFF	1	/* add where that is in its segment */
#define	R_SEG	2	/* the selector of the segment it is in */
#define	R_PCREL	3	/* add how far it is from here */
#define	R_TYPE	0x0f
#define	R_EXT	0x10	/* r_sym is a symbol */

#define	N_BADMAG(x) \e
	((x).a_magic != A_MAGIC && (x).a_magic != A_OBJECT)

#define	N_TXTOFF(x) \e
	((off_t)sizeof(struct exec) + (x).a_nseg * (off_t)sizeof(struct exseg))

/*
 * These take the header with the table of segments, a struct xexec,
 * and call functions because the sizes of the segments have to be
 * summed.
 */
off_t	n_stroff(), n_symoff();

#define	N_STROFF(e) (n_stroff(&e))
#define	N_SYMOFF(e) (n_symoff(&e))
.fi
.PP
The file has six sections:
a header, a table of segments, the contents of each segment in turn,
relocation information, a symbol table and a strings table (in that
order).
Only an object file has relocation.
The last two may be omitted
if the program was loaded
with the `\-s' option
of
.I ld
or if the symbols have been
removed by
.IR strip (1).
.PP
The table has a
.I "struct exseg"
for each of the
.I a_nseg
segments, which are numbered from 1.
A segment has
.I xs_filesz
bytes in the file, made up to an even number there, and
.I xs_memsz
bytes when the program runs, of which those not in the file start
off as all 0.
The size of the header and of the table are not included in any of
the other sizes.
.PP
.IR Programs :
If the magic number in the header is A_MAGIC,
the file is a program.
Its last segment is its data: initialized data, then
uninitialized data, and when it is executed a stack of
.I a_stack
bytes above that, and above the stack what the program asks for with
.IR brk (2).
The segments before it are text, one in the small model and one or
more in the medium, which are not writable by the program;
if other processes are executing the same file,
they will share them.
A running program addresses segment
.I n
with the selector
.IR n "\(mu8 + 7,"
which is fixed when the file is made: the link editor writes it
wherever the program has a segment's selector, and the program is
loaded as it is in the file.
It begins at
.I a_entry
in segment
.IR a_eseg ,
with
.I a_dseg
for its data and stack.
.PP
.IR "Object files" :
If the magic number is A_OBJECT, the file is an object.
It has three segments, the text, the data, and the uninitialized
data (bss), of which nothing is in the file; and after the contents
of the data come
.I xs_nrel
entries of relocation for the text and then as many as the data's
.I xs_nrel
says for the data, each in the order of
.IR r_addr .
An entry is for the word at
.I r_addr
in its segment.
If
.I r_type
has R_EXT,
.I r_sym
is the number of a symbol, counting the first in the symbol table
as 0; if not, it is one of this file's own segments, 1, 2 or 3.
What is done to the word is the rest of
.IR r_type :
for R_OFF, the address of the symbol, or of the start of this file's
part of that segment, is added to it; for R_SEG it becomes the selector
of the segment the symbol is in; and for R_PCREL, which is for a near
call or jump, the address is added and the place of the word
subtracted, the word having in the file what is to be added to the
symbol less the address just after itself.
.PP
The layout of a symbol table entry and the principal flag values
that distinguish symbol types are given in the include file as follows:
.PP
.nf
.ta \w'#define  'u +\w'char  'u +\w'n_name[8];  'u
struct	nlist {
	union {
		char	*n_name;	/* In memory address of symbol name */
		off_t	n_strx;	/* String table offset (file) */
	} n_un;
	u_char	n_type;	/* Type of symbol - see below */
	char	n_ovly;	/* Segment number */
	u_int	n_value;	/* Symbol value */
};

/*
 * Simple values for n_type.
 */
#define	N_UNDF	0x00	/* undefined */
#define	N_ABS	0x01	/* absolute */
#define	N_TEXT	0x02	/* text segment */
#define	N_DATA	0x03	/* data segment */
#define	N_BSS	0x04	/* bss segment */

#define	N_EXT	0x20	/* external (global) bit, OR'ed in */
#define	N_TYPE	0x1f	/* mask for all the type bits */
.fi
.PP
In a program
.I n_ovly
is the number of the segment the symbol is in, which the PDP-11 has
for the number of its overlay, and
.I n_value
its address in that segment.
In an object file
.I n_ovly
is 0 and the address is in the file's own text, data or bss.
If a symbol's type is undefined external,
and the value field is non-zero,
the symbol is interpreted by the loader
.I ld
as
the name of a common region
whose size is indicated by the value of the
symbol.
.PP
The strings table begins with a long, its length in bytes with the
long itself, and the names follow, each ended by a null;
.I n_strx
is where a name begins, counted from the start of the table.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
as(1), ld(1), nm(1), size(1), strip(1), nlist(3)
.SH BUGS
The link editor does not write relocation, so its output cannot be
linked again.
